WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.481
Identity is a foundational component of Zero Trust because access decisions need a reliable understanding of who or what is making a request.

2
00:00:08.481 --> 00:00:15.156
Identities include employees, contractors, partners, applications, services, and automated workloads.

3
00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:18.539
Authentication establishes evidence about an identity.

4
00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:26.930
Stronger methods can reduce reliance on a single password and provide greater confidence, but authentication is only one part of the decision.

5
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.921
Authorization determines what an authenticated identity may do.

6
00:00:30.921 --> 00:00:42.196
Zero Trust encourages organizations to connect permissions to job responsibilities, business need, resource sensitivity, and current risk rather than granting broad access by default.

7
00:00:42.196 --> 00:00:44.621
Context improves the access decision.

8
00:00:44.621 --> 00:00:58.270
Relevant signals may include the requested application, device status, sign-in pattern, location consistency, time, privilege level, data sensitivity, and whether the request aligns with expected work.

9
00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.373
Identity lifecycle management is equally important.

10
00:01:01.373 --> 00:01:07.559
Joiners, movers, and leavers need timely changes so access reflects current responsibilities.

11
00:01:07.559 --> 00:01:13.965
Dormant accounts, outdated group membership, and unreviewed privileges create unnecessary exposure.

12
00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:17.926
Workload identities deserve the same attention as human users.

13
00:01:17.926 --> 00:01:29.560
Applications, services, scripts, and cloud workloads often access sensitive systems, and their credentials and permissions need clear ownership, appropriate limits, and monitoring.

14
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:37.094
Third-party and federated access require governance because the organization may rely on another party's identity processes.

15
00:01:37.094 --> 00:01:44.258
Contracts, technical controls, review procedures, and resource-specific policy help manage that shared responsibility.

16
00:01:44.258 --> 00:01:47.900
Identity-centered security is not identity-only security.

17
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:57.927
Device posture, resource policy, network and application context, monitoring, and data protection still contribute to a complete Zero Trust decision.
